  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe robust upper-level ridge will slip further south, down into the \nfar southern Texas Panhandle or northwestern Texas, on Wednesday. \nTemperatures across the Panhandles will be very slightly less hot, \nbut still expecting widespread 100+ degrees. As it stands now, it \nlooks like a Heat Advisory may eventually be needed for some of the \ntypical hot spots across the area (e.g., Hutchinson county, PDC). \nOtherwise, with the ridge shifting just slightly to the south, this \ncould be enough to realize some slightly better chances for \nthunderstorms to come off the higher terrain of northeastern New \nMexico and move into the northwestern Panhandles. Like the previous \ndays, the inverted-V thermodynamic profile suggests some gusty winds \nwill be possible along with sparse rainfall. \n\nThe upper-level ridge is favored to stay in place or shift slightly \nsouth on Thursday. Temperatures are currently expected to stay \nsimilar to what is forecast on Wednesday. A boundary is favored to \nmove into the northwestern/western Panhandles Thursday afternoon \nwhich could be enough by itself to cause some showers and \nthunderstorms to develop. Additionally, a weak and subtle shortwave \ncould clip eastern Colorado/western Kansas. This could be close \nenough to add additional mid-level forcing to get more thunderstorm \ncoverage. Forecast soundings in the vicinity show inverted-V \nsignatures below relatively rich mid-level moisture with tall/skinny \nCAPE. This environment is favorable for dry microbursts capable of \nproducing strong to severe wind gusts. \n\nGoing into Friday and this coming weekend, the upper-level ridge \nwill retrograde westward causing northwesterly flow aloft to at \nleast briefly position over the Panhandles. As is typical in this \nregime during the summer, this is a good signal for thunderstorms, \nmost likely in the evening to overnight hours. Easterly to \nsoutheasterly surface return flow is favored over this time period \nwhich will result in ample directional shear, reducing the need for \nstronger mid-level winds in order to result in sufficient wind shear \nfor organized thunderstorms. All that's left is the need for \nsufficient instability, which will have to be monitored. The NBM has \nthe highest PoPs in New Mexico and the far western combined \nPanhandles at this time. This would make sense if the winds aloft \nturn northerly or northeasterly too quickly, but while we have \nnorthwesterly flow aloft, thunderstorms will be possible for at \nleast the western and northern halves of the Panhandles.\n\nVanden Bosch",
